# GitHub PR checks

## Gate

Use when the user asks to wait on CI, watch checks, see why a PR is red, or
re-run failed GitHub Actions on a named PR / branch / run.

Never invent a PR number, workflow id, or check name. Resolve from the chat,
the current branch’s open PR, or an explicit URL.

## Prefer MCP

When a GitHub MCP is ready (`global-mcp-first`):

1. Resolve the PR (`pull_request_read` / list / search).
2. List runs or check status (`actions_list`, PR status fields).
3. On failure, pull the failing job logs (`actions_get` / job logs) — short
   excerpt only.
4. Re-run only when the user asked (`actions_run_trigger`).

Do not shell out to `gh` for the same work while MCP works.

## Honesty

- Do **not** claim checks are green, skipped-as-success, or “probably fine”
  without a fresh read.
- Pending ≠ passed. Keep waiting or report still-running jobs by name.
- Distinguish **failed**, **cancelled**, **timed out**, and **skipped**.
- Required vs optional: say when a red check is required for merge if the
  branch protection data is available; otherwise say you do not know.

## Report shape

Keep the operator reply short:

1. PR number + head SHA (when known)
2. Overall conclusion (pending / success / failure)
3. Failing check names + one-line cause when cheap
4. Next action (fix product code, re-run, or wait) — do not invent product
   fixes unless the user also authorized code work

## Refuse

- Do not merge the PR unless the user explicitly asked to merge.
- Do not disable or delete workflows to “make CI green.”
- Do not paste secrets or full multi-megabyte logs into chat.
